From 0679ddb64ecf32848ce53d6d8f360bc5d86efd44 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 19:19:23 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] block: keep raw image size after a discard or write-zeroes at
 the disk's end

imago's File storage (0.2.3) served a discard, unmap write-zeroes or plain
write-zeroes whose range reaches the end of the file by truncating the file
to the start of the range (File::try_discard_by_truncate). For a raw image
the file length is the disk size, so the disk shrank: the guest kept the old
capacity until it was restarted, then saw a smaller device than the
filesystem on it. mke2fs zeroes the last 64 KiB of the device it formats
(old MD RAID superblock wipe), so a whole-disk ext4 made in the guest lost
exactly 64 KiB of image and failed to mount on the next boot ("filesystem
larger than device"); fstrim/blkdiscard of a whole-disk filesystem whose
last blocks are free does the same. A GPT disk such as steamos.img is not
hit in practice: the backup GPT occupies the last sectors, so no partition
discard reaches the end of the file.

Upstream state: fixed in imago 0.2.5 (hreitz/imago d48b76e1 "file: keep
image size after tail discard": truncate, then set the length back).
libkrun v1.19.6 pins imago 0.2.3; libkrun main (at the time) pins 0.2.4,
which still truncates. This takes imago 0.2.5 (Cargo.lock: imago, plus its
new futures/maybe-async/slab deps; bincode/unty/virtue dropped) and adds a
regression test. No code change in libkrun itself; nothing changes for
requests that do not reach the end of the disk.

Test: virtio::block::device::tests::
raw_image_keeps_size_after_tail_discard_and_write_zeroes opens a raw image
through Block::new, sends write-zeroes, unmap write-zeroes and discard for
the last 64 KiB, and checks the file size and the capacity on reopen. Fails
on imago 0.2.3 (4194304 -> 4128768 bytes), passes on 0.2.5.
